//! Computational group theory: **permutation groups**.
//!
//! A [`Permutation`] is a bijection of the point set `{0, 1, …, n−1}` stored as
//! an images array; a [`PermutationGroup`] is the subgroup of `S_n` generated by
//! a list of them. From a list of generators this module computes orbits with
//! Schreier vectors, a base and strong generating set by the Schreier–Sims
//! algorithm, and from that BSGS the exact group order as an arbitrary-precision
//! [`rug::Integer`], membership testing by sifting, uniform random elements, and
//! (below a hard cap) full element enumeration.
//!
//! # Two conventions to read before anything else
//!
//! **Points are 0-based.** `Permutation::from_images(vec![1, 0, 2])` is the
//! transposition swapping points `0` and `1`. Group-theory literature, GAP and
//! the ATLAS are all 1-based, so transcribing a generator by hand is the most
//! likely way to get a wrong answer here;
//! [`Permutation::from_cycles_one_based`] exists so that a cycle copied out of a
//! paper can be typed in unchanged.
//!
//! **Composition is left-to-right.** `p.compose(&q)` applies `p` *first* and `q`
//! *second*:
//!
//! ```text
//!     (p ∘ q)(i)  =  q(p(i))            equivalently   i^(p·q) = (i^p)^q
//! ```
//!
//! This is GAP's convention and the one that makes the exponential notation
//! `i^g` used throughout the stabilizer-chain literature read left to right. It
//! is the **opposite** of the `f ∘ g = f(g(x))` convention used for functions in
//! analysis, and the opposite of what `nalgebra`-style matrix code would do.
//! Every product in this module — Schreier generators `u_β · x · u_{β^x}^{-1}`,
//! the coset factorisation of an element, the transversal walk — is written in
//! that order, and [`Permutation::compose`] is the only place the order is
//! decided. If you mix conventions you get a group of the right *order* whose
//! elements are the inverses of the ones you wanted, which is exactly the kind
//! of bug the Mathieu-group order tests in this module exist to catch.
//!
//! # What is here
//!
//! * [`Permutation`] — images array, [`compose`](Permutation::compose),
//!   [`inverse`](Permutation::inverse), [`identity`](Permutation::identity),
//!   [`order`](Permutation::order) (arbitrary precision — Landau's function
//!   outruns `u64` by degree ≈ 180), [`cycles`](Permutation::cycles),
//!   [`cycle_type`](Permutation::cycle_type), [`sign`](Permutation::sign),
//!   [`degree`](Permutation::degree).
//! * [`PermutationGroup`] — built from generators; [`orbit`](PermutationGroup::orbit)
//!   and [`orbits`](PermutationGroup::orbits), [`stabilizer_chain`](PermutationGroup::stabilizer_chain),
//!   [`order`](PermutationGroup::order), [`contains`](PermutationGroup::contains),
//!   [`sift`](PermutationGroup::sift), [`random_element`](PermutationGroup::random_element),
//!   [`elements`](PermutationGroup::elements).
//! * [`Orbit`] — the orbit of a point together with its **Schreier vector** and
//!   the transversal derived from it.
//! * [`StabilizerChain`] — the base, the strong generators at each level, and
//!   each level's basic orbit, all exposed rather than hidden behind `order()`.
//! * Standard constructors: [`symmetric`], [`alternating`], [`cyclic`],
//!   [`dihedral`], [`trivial`].
//!
//! # What is *not* here, and why
//!
//! This pass is deliberately the permutation-group foundation and nothing else.
//! None of the following are implemented, and no entry point silently
//! approximates them:
//!
//! * **Finitely-presented groups and Todd–Coxeter coset enumeration.** A
//!   presentation `⟨X | R⟩` is a different object with different algorithms
//!   (and no termination guarantee); there is no `FpGroup` type here at all.
//! * **Ordinary character tables** are no longer absent, but they are not
//!   *here*: conjugacy classes and the Dixon–Schneider table live in
//!   [`crate::character`], which takes a [`PermutationGroup`] as its input and
//!   caps `|G|` because it enumerates. Modular representation theory and Brauer
//!   characters remain unimplemented.
//! * **Matrix groups over `GF(q)`.** `alkahest` has no finite-field type that
//!   this module could be generic over, so a matrix group would have to invent
//!   one. Permutation groups only.
//! * **Group cohomology**, group extensions, and the solvable/polycyclic
//!   machinery (`PcGroup`).
//! * **Group-theoretic structure beyond the chain**: no Sylow subgroups, no
//!   normal-closure/derived-series/composition-series computations, no subgroup
//!   lattice, no automorphism groups, no backtrack search (set/partition
//!   stabilizers, centralizers, intersections). `contains` and `order` come
//!   from the chain; everything that needs backtracking is absent. Conjugacy
//!   classes are in [`crate::character`] and are found by enumerating the
//!   group, not by backtrack search, which is why they are capped there.
//! * **Randomised (Monte-Carlo) Schreier–Sims.** The implementation here is the
//!   deterministic Schreier-generator algorithm: always correct, and
//!   comfortably fast at the degrees this module admits, but not the
//!   near-linear-time algorithm used for degrees in the millions.
//!
//! # Scope limits that are enforced rather than documented
//!
//! * The stabilizer chain stores an **explicit transversal permutation per
//!   orbit point**, so its memory is `O(Σ_i |Δ_i| · n)` words — for `S_n` that
//!   is about `4n³` bytes. Degrees above [`MAX_BSGS_DEGREE`] are therefore
//!   refused ([`GroupError::DegreeTooLargeForBsgs`]) rather than allowed to
//!   exhaust memory. Orbit computation itself has no such limit; only the chain
//!   does. Lifting the cap means storing Schreier vectors alone and
//!   reconstructing `u_β` on demand, which trades the memory for a factor of
//!   `|Δ_i|` in time.
//! * Element enumeration refuses above [`DEFAULT_ELEMENT_CAP`] (and at all
//!   above [`MAX_ELEMENT_CAP`]) with [`GroupError::EnumerationTooLarge`]. A
//!   group of order `10^20` has a perfectly computable order and no listable
//!   element set, and those two facts must not be confused.

